1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,624
A bank statement is a picture of your money.

2
00:00:02,949 --> 00:00:07,314
But a picture is not an answer. Meet DocsAnalyzer.

3
00:00:07,639 --> 00:00:10,670
Turn statements into transactions you can explore.

4
00:00:10,948 --> 00:00:12,980
Start with a PDF or spreadsheet.

5
00:00:13,363 --> 00:00:16,904
Here, we are using the built-in sample, not a real customer's

6
00:00:16,904 --> 00:00:21,014
account. Move from rows on a page to a searchable table.

7
00:00:21,397 --> 00:00:24,555
Find a payment. Group transactions by payee.

8
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:27,353
See money coming in and money going out.

9
00:00:27,411 --> 00:00:31,521
Then check the structure: opening balance, plus credits, minus

10
00:00:31,521 --> 00:00:34,041
debits, equals closing balance.

11
00:00:34,551 --> 00:00:36,757
A mismatch deserves a closer look.

12
00:00:37,199 --> 00:00:42,063
DocsAnalyzer's site describes two ways to work: in the browser,

13
00:00:42,063 --> 00:00:44,315
or with an offline desktop app.

14
00:00:44,757 --> 00:00:47,531
Check the privacy details for the mode you choose.

15
00:00:47,915 --> 00:00:51,606
Less hunting through pages. More room to understand the numbers.

16
00:00:51,931 --> 00:00:54,996
Try the sample at DocsAnalyzer dot com.

17
00:00:55,321 --> 00:00:57,260
From statement to understanding.